---
id: 021602d5-4710-4bfa-8415-c5e44d473384
title: Groin Hernia
---

# Groin Hernia

- **Definition of hernia** - protrusion, bulge or projection of an organ (or part of an organ) through the body wall that normally contains it
- **Definition of groin hernia** - herniation through the groin area, encompassing inguinal hernia and femoral hernia
- **Epidemiology:**
  - <u>Prevalence</u> - 27-43% in M, 3-6% in F
- **Risk factors for inguinal hernias:**
  - **Intrinsic risk factors:**
    - <u>Demographic</u> - male sex (8-10x risk), extremes of age (indirect hernia in the young, and direct hernia in the old)
    - <u>FHx</u> - +ve FHx of inguinal hernia in 1st-degree relatives
    - <u>PMH</u> - collagen metabolism disorders, previous contralateral hernia
  - **Acquired risk factors:**
    - <u>Hx of voiding Sx and BPH</u> - excessive straining during voiding causes increased intra-abdominal presure
    - <u>Hx of prostatectomy</u> - esp. open (4x risk), but also occurs in TURP (as aaccess via MPO)
    - <u>Hx of chronic constipation</u> - evidence is low
    - <u>Hx of pulmonary disease</u> - evidence is very low
    - <u>Hx of peritoneal dialysis</u> - increased intra-abdominal pressure by PD fluid
    - <u>Obesity</u> - evidence controversial, seems to be a RF and a protective factor mechanistically
- **Classification of groin hernia** - classified into 1) inguinal hernia, and 2) femoral hernia:
  - **Inguinal hernia** - medial and above the PT:
    - **Indirect inguinal hernia** - origin of hernia sac lateral to inferior epigastric vessels (outside Hesselbach's triangle), and enter the deep inguinal ring due to dysfunctional closure of internal inguinal ring (patent processus vaginalis)
    - **Direct inguinal hernia** - musculofascial weakness (usually over floor of inguinal canal), causing herniation medial to inferior epigastric vessels
    - **Pataloon hernia** - presence of both direct and indirect hernia
    - **Sliding hernia** - internal organ (usually sigmoid) forms part of the wall of the hernia sac
    - **Amyland hernia** - type of indirect hernia involving herniation of the vermiform appendix
    - **Littre hernia** - herniation of the meckel's diverticulum
    - **Ritcher's hernia** - herniation of the anti-mesenteric portion of the intestinal wall, causing incarceration w/o IO
    - **Madyl's hernia** - herniation of two bowel loops, causing closed-loop obstruction of the intra-abdominal portion

    
    ![](../img/types_of_inguinal_hernia.png)
  - **Femoral hernia** (\< 10% of all groin hernias) - inferior to the inguinal ligament and protrude through the femoral ring
- **Pathophysiology of groin hernia:**
  - **Myopectineal oriface theory:**
    - <u>Principles</u> - propensity of hernia to form over the inguino-femoral region because the MPO is not re-inforced by a muscular layer
    - <u>Borders of MPO</u>:
      - Superiorly - Arching fibres of the inferior oblique and transversalis
      - Inferiorly - Cooper's ligament (pectineal line)
      - Medially - Rectus abdominus and fibrous rectus sheath
      - Laterally - Iliopsoas muscle

      
      ![](../img/myopectineal_oriface.png)
  - **Progressive weakening of the musculofascial tissue** - due to connective tissue abnormalities, abdominal wall injuries and chronic over-stretching of the musculoaponeurotic structures
  - **Increased intra-abdominal pressure** - causes herniation sac to protrude through the weakened fascia
- **Clinical features of groin hernias:**
  - **Groin heaviness or discomfort** - groin discomfort in association with rise in intra-abdominal pressure causing **constriction of contents in the hernia sac**:
    - <u>Onset</u> - gradual in onset
    - <u>Exacerbating factors</u> - increased intra-abdominal pressure (e.g. heavy lifting, straining, prolonged standing)
    - <u>Relieving factors</u> - rest or lying down
    - <u>Severity</u> - usually mild (moderate-to-severe pain suggestive of incarceration or strangulation)
    - <u>Timing</u> - usually more pronounced at the end of the day after prolonged activities
  - **Visible groin mass** - may or may not be present, especially in context of obesity, but may be a/w cough impulse
  - **Features of strangulation:**
    - <u>Fever and systemic upset</u> - localised peritonitis, but never generalised as the pathology is encased in the hernia sac
    - <u>Features suggestive of ischaemic/ inflammatory process</u> - groin pain disproportionate to simple hernia and erythema over skin
    - <u>Features suggestive to IO</u> - abdominal pain, abdominal distension, constipation, N/V (late feature in LBO)
- **P/E:**
- **Dx** - clinical diagnosis based on Hx and P/E
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Asymptomatic or mildly symptomatic or pregnancy</u> - watchuful waiting
    - <u>Symptomatic hernia</u> - surgical repair
  - **Classification of surgical repair:**
    - **Approach** - open vs minimally invasive:
      - **Open repair** - repair the hernia defect anteriorly or posteriorly either by tension free mesh repair, or primary tissue nonmesh repair:
        - <u>Tension free mesh repair</u> (Hernioplasty) - e.g. Lichstenstein (anterior), Stoppa (posterior)
        - <u>Primary tissue approximation non-mesh repair</u> (Herniorrhaphy) - Bassini, Shouldice

        
        ![](../img/open_techniques_for_hernia_repair.png)
      - **Minimally invasive repair** - repair the hernia defect posteriorly through laparoscopic (or robotic) approaches:
        - <u>Totally extraperitoneal patch repair</u> (TEP)
        - <u>Extended view TEP</u> (eTEP)
        - <u>Transabdominal pre-peritoneal patch reapair</u> (TAPP)
    - **Use of mesh** - tension-free mesh repair vs tissue repair:
      - <u>Mesh repair</u> - use of mesh to close the defect after herniotomy
      - <u>Tissue repair</u> - use of sutures to pull the edges of the hernia defect to close it resulting in tension and **increased recurrence rate** (hence tension repair, except Shouldice, which is considered tension free)
  - **Surgical approach to inguinal hernia repair** - meta-analysis suggests <u>advantages to laparoscopic over open repair</u>, hence laparoscopic is preferred whenever feasible:
    - **Evidence of superiority of laproscopic approaches** - 2021 network meta-analysis comparing laparoscopic repair and Lichtenstein:
      - <u>Reduced early post-operative pain</u> - RR \[0.36\]
      - <u>Faster recovery and earlier return to work and activities</u> - weighted mean difference \[-3.3 - -3.6\]
    - **Considerations for technique to hernia repair:**
      - <u>Hernia factors</u> - characteristics of hernia
      - <u>Patient factor</u> - patient comorbidities contraindicating laproscopic approach
      - <u>Surgeon factor</u> - surgeon experience
    - **C/I for laparoscopic approach** - related to anatomical reasons or intolerability to GA:
      - **Recurrent hernia with prior laparoscopic repair** - pre-peritoneal space is difficult to access due to presence of fibrous tissue
      - **Irreducible inguinoscrotal hernia** - difficult to reduce intra-operatively as incarcerated components are difficult to manipulate laproscopically
      - **Prior surgery of lower pre-peritoneal space** - e.g. prostatectomy, hysterectomy, cesarean section, laparotomy via lower midline incision (obliteration of pre-peritoneal space from prior surgeries by adhesions)
      - **Patients with ascites** - entry of ascites or even fistula formation at trocar sites
      - **Intolerance to general anaesthesia** - laparoscopic repair C/I and open repair is performed under LA

      
      ![](../img/tx_algo_for_uncomplicaated_groin_hernia.png)
  - **Litchtenstein repair** - open anterior tension-free mesh repair:
    - <u>Procedure</u> - Herniotomy and mesh repair with flat mesh placed anterior to transversalis fascia
    - <u>Indications</u> - suitable for most inguinal hernia (**gold standard**), but not for femoral hernia, as mesh does not cover femoral ring
  - **Shouldice repair** - extremely specialised non-mesh repair with lowest recurrence rate among non-mesh repairs (considered tension-free)
- **Complications of hernia replair:**
  - **Immediate** - AROU or bladder injury
  - **Early:**
    - **Infection** - wound or mesh infection
    - **Seroma/ Haematoma** - collecting of fluid in the dead space that remains once hernia sac is reduced
  - **Late:**
    - **Chronic postoperative inguinal pain** (10-12%) - bothersome moderate pain that impacts daily activities lasting \>= 3 mo postoperatively
    - **Ischaemic orchitis** - resulting in testicular atrophy
